BODY_BEGIN
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000001 "=head1 NAME"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000002 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000003 "Test::Warn - Perl extension to test methods for warnings"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000004 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000005 "=head1 SYNOPSIS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000006 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000007 "  use Test::Warn;"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000008 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000009 "  warning_is    {foo(-dri => \"/\")} \"Unknown Parameter 'dri'\", \"dri != dir gives warning\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000010 "  warnings_are  {bar(1,1)} [\"Width very small\", \"Height very small\"];"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000011 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000012 "  warning_is    {add(2,2)} undef, \"No warnings for calc 2+2\"; # or"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000013 "  warnings_are  {add(2,2)} [],    \"No warnings for calc 2+2\"; # whichever reads better :-)"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000014 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000015 "  warning_like  {foo(-dri => \"/\")} qr/unknown param/i, \"an unknown parameter test\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000016 "  warnings_like {bar(1,1)} [qr/width.*small/i, qr/height.*small/i];"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000017 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000018 "  warning_is    {foo()} {carped => \"didn't find the right parameters\"};"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000019 "  warnings_like {foo()} [qr/undefined/,qr/undefined/,{carped => qr/no result/i}];"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000020 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000021 "  warning_like {foo(undef)}                 'uninitialized';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000022 "  warning_like {bar(file => '/etc/passwd')} 'io';"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000023 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000024 "  warning_like {eval q/\"$x\"; $x;/}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000025 "               [qw/void uninitialized/],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000026 "               \"some warnings at compile time\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000027 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000028 "  warnings_exist {...} [qr/expected warning/], \"Expected warning is thrown\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000029 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000030 "=head1 DESCRIPTION"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000031 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000032 "A good style of Perl programming calls for a lot of diverse regression tests."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000033 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000034 "This module provides a few convenience methods for testing warning based-code."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000035 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000036 "If you are not already familiar with the L<Test::More> manpage"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000037 "now would be the time to go take a look."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000038 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000039 "=head2 FUNCTIONS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000040 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000041 "=over 4"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000042 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000043 "=item B<warning_is> I<BLOCK STRING, TEST_NAME>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000044 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000045 "Tests that BLOCK gives the specified warning exactly once."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000046 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000047 "The test fails if the BLOCK warns more than once or does not warn at all."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000048 "If the string is undef, then the test succeeds if the BLOCK doesn't"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000049 "give any warning."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000050 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000051 "Another way to say that there are no warnings in the block"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000052 "is:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000053 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000054 "  warnings_are {foo()} [], \"no warnings\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000055 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000056 "If you want to test for a warning given by Carp"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000057 "you have to write something like:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000058 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000059 "  warning_is {carp \"msg\"} {carped => 'msg'}, \"Test for a carped warning\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000060 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000061 "The test will fail if a \"normal\" warning is found instead of a \"carped\" one."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000062 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000063 "Note: C<warn \"foo\"> would print something like C<foo at -e line 1>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000064 "This method ignores everything after the \"at\". Thus to match this warning"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000065 "you would have to call C<< warning_is {warn \"foo\"} \"foo\", \"Foo succeeded\" >>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000066 "If you need to test for a warning at an exact line,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000067 "try something like:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000068 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000069 "  warning_like {warn \"foo\"} qr/at XYZ.dat line 5/"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000070 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000071 "Warn messages with a trailing newline (like C<warn \"foo\\n\">) don't produce the C<at -e line 1> message by Perl."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000072 "Up to Test::Warn 0.30 such warning weren't supported by C<< warning_is {warn \"foo\\n\"} \"foo\\n\" >>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000073 "Starting with version 0.31 they are supported, but also marked as experimental."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000074 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000075 "L<C<warning_is()>|/warning_is-BLOCK-STRING-TEST_NAME> and L<C<warnings_are()>|/warnings_are-BLOCK-ARRAYREF-TEST_NAME>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000076 "are only aliases to the same method.  So you also could write"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000077 "C<< warning_is {foo()} [], \"no warning\" >> or something similar."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000078 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000079 "I decided to give two methods the same name to improve readability."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000080 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000081 "A true value is returned if the test succeeds, false otherwise."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000082 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000083 "The test name is optional, but recommended."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000084 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000085 "=item B<warnings_are> I<BLOCK ARRAYREF, TEST_NAME>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000086 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000087 "Tests to see that BLOCK gives exactly the specified warnings."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000088 "The test fails if the warnings from BLOCK are not exactly the ones in ARRAYREF."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000089 "If the ARRAYREF is equal to C<< [] >>,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000090 "then the test succeeds if the BLOCK doesn't give any warning."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000091 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000092 "Please read also the notes to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000093 "L<C<warning_is()>|/warning_is-BLOCK-STRING-TEST_NAME>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000094 "as these methods are only aliases."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000095 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000096 "If you want more than one test for carped warnings, try this:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000097 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000098 "  warnings_are {carp \"c1\"; carp \"c2\"} {carped => ['c1','c2'];"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000099 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000100 "or"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000101 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000102 "  warnings_are {foo()} [\"Warning 1\", {carped => [\"Carp 1\", \"Carp 2\"]}, \"Warning 2\"];"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000103 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000104 "Note that C<< {carped => ...} >> must always be a hash ref."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000105 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000106 "=item B<warning_like> I<BLOCK REGEXP, TEST_NAME>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000107 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000108 "Tests that BLOCK gives exactly one warning and it can be matched by"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000109 "the given regexp."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000110 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000111 "If the string is undef, then the tests succeeds if the BLOCK doesn't"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000112 "give any warning."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000113 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000114 "The REGEXP is matched against the whole warning line,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000115 "which in general has the form C<< \"WARNING at __FILE__ line __LINE__\" >>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000116 "So you can check for a warning in the file C<Foo.pm> on line 5 with:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000117 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000118 "  warning_like {bar()} qr/at Foo.pm line 5/, \"Testname\""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000119 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000120 "I don't know whether it makes sense to do such a test :-("
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000121 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000122 "However, you should be prepared as a matching with C<'at'>, C<'file'>, C<'\\d'>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000123 "or similar will always pass."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000124 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000125 "Consider C<< qr/^foo/ >> if you want to test for warning C<\"foo something\"> in file F<foo.pl>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000126 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000127 "You can also write the regexp in a string as C<\"/.../\">"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000128 "instead of using the C<< qr/.../ >> syntax."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000129 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000130 "Note that the slashes are important in the string,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000131 "as strings without slashes are reserved for warning categories"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000132 "(to match warning categories as can be seen in the perllexwarn man page)."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000133 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000134 "Similar to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000135 "L<< C<warning_is()>|/warning_is-BLOCK-STRING-TEST_NAME >> and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000136 "L<< C<warnings_are()>|/warnings_are-BLOCK-ARRAYREF-TEST_NAME >>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000137 "you can test for warnings via C<carp> with:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000138 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000139 "  warning_like {bar()} {carped => qr/bar called too early/i};"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000140 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000141 "Similar to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000142 "L<< C<warning_is()>|/warning_is-BLOCK-STRING-TEST_NAME >> and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000143 "L<< C<warnings_are()>|/warnings_are-BLOCK-ARRAYREF-TEST_NAME >>,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000144 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000145 "L<< C<warning_like()>|/warning_like-BLOCK-REGEXP-TEST_NAME >> and"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000146 "L<< C<warnings_like()>|/warnings_like-BLOCK-ARRAYREF-TEST_NAME >>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000147 "are only aliases to the same methods."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000148 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000149 "A true value is returned if the test succeeds, false otherwise."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000150 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000151 "The test name is optional, but recommended."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000152 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000153 "=item B<warning_like> I<BLOCK STRING, TEST_NAME>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000154 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000155 "Tests whether a BLOCK gives exactly one warning of the passed category."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000156 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000157 "The categories are grouped in a tree,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000158 "like it is expressed in L<perllexwarn>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000159 "Also see L</BUGS AND LIMITATIONS>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000160 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000161 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000162 "Thanks to the grouping in a tree,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000163 "it's possible to test simply for an 'io' warning,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000164 "instead of testing for a 'closed|exec|layer|newline|pipe|unopened' warning."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000165 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000166 "Note, that warnings occurring at compile time"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000167 "can only be caught in an eval block. So"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000168 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000169 "  warning_like {eval q/\"$x\"; $x;/}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000170 "                [qw/void uninitialized/],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000171 "                \"some warnings at compile time\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000172 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000173 "will work, while it wouldn't work without the eval."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000174 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000175 "Note, that it isn't possible yet,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000176 "to test for own categories,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000177 "created with L<warnings::register>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000178 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000179 "=item B<warnings_like> I<BLOCK ARRAYREF, TEST_NAME>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000180 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000181 "Tests to see that BLOCK gives exactly the number of the specified"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000182 "warnings, in the defined order."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000183 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000184 "Please read also the notes to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000185 "L<< C<warning_like()>|/warning_like-BLOCK-REGEXP-TEST_NAME >>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000186 "as these methods are only aliases."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000187 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000188 "Similar to"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000189 "L<< C<warnings_are()>|/warnings_are-BLOCK-ARRAYREF-TEST_NAME >>,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000190 "you can test for multiple warnings via C<carp>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000191 "and for warning categories, too:"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000192 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000193 "  warnings_like {foo()}"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000194 "                [qr/bar warning/,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000195 "                 qr/bar warning/,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000196 "                 {carped => qr/bar warning/i},"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000197 "                 'io'"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000198 "                ],"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000199 "                \"I hope you'll never have to write a test for so many warnings :-)\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000200 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000201 "=item B<warnings_exist> I<BLOCK STRING|ARRAYREF, TEST_NAME>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000202 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000203 "Same as warning_like, but will C<< warn() >> all warnings that do not match the supplied regex/category,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000204 "instead of registering an error. Use this test when you just want to make sure that specific"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000205 "warnings were generated, and couldn't care less if other warnings happened in the same block"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000206 "of code."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000207 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000208 "  warnings_exist {...} [qr/expected warning/], \"Expected warning is thrown\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000209 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000210 "  warnings_exist {...} ['uninitialized'], \"Expected warning is thrown\";"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000211 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000212 "=back"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000213 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000214 "=head2 EXPORT"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000215 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000216 "C<warning_is>,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000217 "C<warnings_are>,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000218 "C<warning_like>,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000219 "C<warnings_like>,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000220 "C<warnings_exist> by default."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000221 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000222 "=head1 BUGS AND LIMITATIONS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000223 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000224 "Category check is done as C<< qr/category_name/ >>. In some case this works, like for"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000225 "category C<'uninitialized'>. For C<'utf8'> it does not work. Perl does not have a list"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000226 "of warnings, so it is not possible to generate one for C<Test::Warn>."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000227 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000228 "If you want to add a warning to a category, send a pull request. Modifications"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000229 "should be done to C<< %warnings_in_category >>. You should look into perl source to check"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000230 "how warning is looking exactly."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000231 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000232 "Please note that warnings with newlines inside are very awkward."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000233 "The only sensible way to handle them is to use the C<warning_like> or"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000234 "C<warnings_like> methods. The background is that there is no"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000235 "really safe way to distinguish between warnings with newlines and a"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000236 "stacktrace."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000237 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000238 "If a method has its own warn handler,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000239 "overwriting C<$SIG{__WARN__}>,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000240 "my test warning methods won't get these warnings."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000241 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000242 "The C<warning_like BLOCK CATEGORY, TEST_NAME> method isn't fully tested."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000243 "Please take note if you use this this calling style,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000244 "and report any bugs you find."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000245 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000246 "=head2 XS warnings"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000247 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000248 "As described in https://rt.cpan.org/Ticket/Display.html?id=42070&results=3c71d1b101a730e185691657f3b02f21 or https://github.com/hanfried/test-warn/issues/1 XS warnings might not be caught."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000249 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000250 "=head1 SEE ALSO"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000251 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000252 "Have a look to the similar L<Test::Exception> module. L<Test::Trap>"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000253 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000254 "=head1 THANKS"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000255 ""
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000256 "Many thanks to Adrian Howard, chromatic and Michael G. Schwern,"
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000257 "who have given me a lot of ideas."
NSQ_SOURCE_LINE 00000258 ""
